\includegraphics[width=0.49\textwidth]{Figure_003-a.pdf}
\includegraphics[width=0.49\textwidth]{Figure_003-b.pdf}
\caption{Distributions of the invariant mass of the \PJGy meson candidate and the leading lepton combination, in the leading $\mu$ (left) and leading e (right) combinations. Processes are normalized to their theoretical cross sections. The simulation assumes a value of $m_\PQt=172.5\GeV$. The lower panel shows the ratio of the number of events observed in data to the number expected from simulation. Two points are not visible in the lower panel of the left plot and one for the right plot, as it would require to enlarge the $y$-axis range up to 5.5 and 18 units, respectively.}
\label{fig:jpsil}
\end{figure}


\section{Extraction of the top quark mass}
\label{sec:measurement}

\subsection{Fitting procedure}
\label{subsec:measurement:procedure}

Since no significant differences are observed between $\PJGy+\mu$ and $\PJGy+\Pe$ events, no further distinction is made on the flavor of the leading lepton.
In associating the leading lepton to a \PJGy meson in a \ttbar event, there are configurations where both particles arise from the same top quark decay chain or from different top quarks (referred to as ``wrong pairings'').
The right and wrong pairings are considered simultaneously in the analysis. While wrong pairings are less sensitive to $m_\PQt$, they remain weakly correlated with it.

